<section class="reference-section" id="gargi-yajnavalkya"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Gargi and Yajnavalkya · The Great Debate</h2><a href="/gargi-yajnavalkya.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Brihadaranyaka Upanishad · Gita Press, Gorakhpur.</strong> Owner-supplied Sanskrit text with Shankara-bhashya and Hindi explanation. This is an original condensed English reader, not a translation. The private scan is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>3.1.1–2</strong><small>King Janaka’s sacrifice, the thousand cows with gold and Yajnavalkya’s instruction to take them.</small></li><li><strong>3.6.1</strong><small>Gargi’s first exchange: the rising sequence of questions beginning with what the world is woven upon.</small></li><li><strong>3.6.1</strong><small>The warning at the limit of treating the ultimate as one more object in a cosmological chain.</small></li><li><strong>3.8.1–2</strong><small>Gargi rises again, announces two questions and compares them to a warrior’s arrows.</small></li><li><strong>3.8.3–6</strong><small>Her first question about what is above, below and between, including past, present and future; Yajnavalkya answers that all is woven in akasha.</small></li><li><strong>3.8.7–9</strong><small>The second question and the description of the Imperishable, including the ordering of sun, moon, time and rivers.</small></li><li><strong>3.8.10–11</strong><small>The unseen seer, unheard hearer, unthought thinker and unknown knower; Gargi’s judgment before the assembly.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> Sections 3.6 and 3.8 are separate encounters; other debates intervene. The narrative condenses the larger Brahmodya of chapter 3 and does not present every interlocutor or argument. “Space” is used as an accessible gloss for <em>akasha</em>, while the Sanskrit term is retained where its philosophical range matters. The reflection question is editorial.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated interpretations used once each. Gargi, Yajnavalkya, Janaka, the assembly, court, clothing and landscape are imagined. The woven water, air and sky are explicitly conceptual; Brahman and the Imperishable are not personified.</p></div></section>

